1
00:00:00,230 --> 00:00:01,690
Why is the sky blue?

2
00:00:01,990 --> 00:00:04,500
The answer is hiding inside sunlight.

3
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:06,890
Sunlight looks white, but it's really

4
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:08,610
every color of the rainbow traveling

5
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:09,700
together.

6
00:00:09,930 --> 00:00:11,380
Each color is a wave.

7
00:00:11,690 --> 00:00:13,600
Red waves are long and lazy.

8
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,790
Blue waves are short and choppy.

9
00:00:16,230 --> 00:00:18,310
When sunlight hits the air, tiny molecules

10
00:00:18,310 --> 00:00:20,110
bounce the short blue waves in every

11
00:00:20,170 --> 00:00:21,000
direction.

12
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,240
The long red waves mostly pass straight

13
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:24,000
through.

14
00:00:24,230 --> 00:00:26,310
Blue scatters about six times more than

15
00:00:26,310 --> 00:00:28,960
red, so blue reaches your eyes from every

16
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:29,900
part of the sky.

17
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:31,900
But here's the odd parts.

18
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:33,900
Violet scatters even more than blue.

19
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:36,400
So why isn't the sky violet?

20
00:00:36,630 --> 00:00:38,400
The sun sends out less of it.

21
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:41,460
The upper atmosphere absorbs some, and our

22
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:43,400
eyes are less sensitive to it.

23
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:45,900
At sunset light crosses far more air.

24
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:48,000
The blue gets scattered away and red is

25
00:00:48,230 --> 00:00:48,900
what's left.

26
00:00:49,130 --> 00:00:50,460
Blue sky by day.

27
00:00:50,460 --> 00:00:52,400
Red sky by evening.

28
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:54,400
Same sunlight, different path.

29
00:00:54,630 --> 00:00:56,900
Follow for more quick science.
